artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a compact, connected, smoothly immersed surface with boundary, and assume:

(i) $\Sigma$ has constant mean curvature (cmc), i.e. its mean curvature $H$ is constant on $\Sigma$;

(ii) the boundary $\partial \Sigma$ is a round circle in some plane of $\mathbb{R}^3$;

(iii) $\Sigma$ is star-shaped, meaning there exists a point $p_0 \in \mathbb{R}^3$ such that every ray from $p_0$ meets $\Sigma$ in at most one point (equivalently, $\Sigma$ is a radial graph over a domain of the unit sphere centered at $p_0$).

Question: Must $\Sigma$ necessarily be either a flat planar disk bounded by the circle, or a spherical cap bounded by the circle?

